WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.350
Good morning Hank; it’s Wednesday, May 18th,
2011, also known as Button Your Button Day.

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:08.269
Hank, in the last 24 hours I finished the
big revision of my novel and I also watched

00:00:08.269 --> 00:00:12.179
your poetic commencement address, and it made
me think a lot about education and why we’re

00:00:12.179 --> 00:00:13.179
alive and stuff.

00:00:13.179 --> 00:00:16.730
Right, so when we think about education we
often think about a soft/hard continuum. On

00:00:16.730 --> 00:00:21.000
the far edge of hard, you have mathematics
which, you know, there’s only one right

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.109
answer, and everything’s about numbers and
data. And then on the far edge of the soft

00:00:24.109 --> 00:00:27.630
side you have the study of literature, where
everything’s about, you know, what it means

00:00:27.630 --> 00:00:33.220
to be a person in a world full of other people
and how to understand ourselves in history

00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:34.530
and all that boring stuff.

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.250
You know what I’m talking about, Hank: history’s
pretty soft, physics is pretty hard, biology

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:42.330
is somewhere in the middle because, you know,
it involves adorable baby pigs but they’re

00:00:42.330 --> 00:00:45.690
dead and you have to dissect them. Psychology
thinks that it’s hard but it’s actually

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:48.330
soft; economics thinks that it’s soft but
it’s actually hard, you know what I’m

00:00:48.330 --> 00:00:49.330
talking about!

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:54.220
Right, except in my opinion, Hank, that continuum
is complete and utter... Warner Chilcott.

00:00:54.220 --> 00:00:57.250
So Hank, when you study mathematics, you’re
really studying two things: first you’re

00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:00.760
studying mathematical language. So the simplest
kind of mathematical language is that when

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:04.320
we talk about the number seven we’re not
only talking about seven donuts or seven pairs

00:01:04.320 --> 00:01:08.530
of pants, we’re talking about the abstracted
idea of seven. And then when you get to algebra,

00:01:08.530 --> 00:01:12.250
the language of mathematics suddenly involves
letters. And the language of math gets more

00:01:12.250 --> 00:01:15.530
and more complex as you learn, which is why,
when I read something by Brotherhood 2.0 Resident

00:01:15.530 --> 00:01:17.970
Mathematician Daniel Biss, I feel illiterate.

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:20.410
So that’s the first thing you’re doing
when you study math. The second thing is that

00:01:20.410 --> 00:01:22.980
you’re attempting to understand the universe,
right? I mean, I don’t want to overstate

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.930
the matter, but that’s basically what we’re
doing. Ultimately, the reason that we have

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:30.159
to know that the square root of four is two
is because it helps us to build cathedrals

00:01:30.159 --> 00:01:34.200
and think about space and make out with people,
but more on that in a second.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:37.250
Right, so we study math for the twin reasons
of wanting to learn mathematical language

00:01:37.250 --> 00:01:40.560
and wanting to understand our place in the
universe, which turns out to be precisely

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:45.720
why we also study literature: to learn about
language and to understand our place in the

00:01:45.720 --> 00:01:48.810
universe. Which by the way, Hank, gets to
the thing that make me angriest in the entire

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.760
world, which is when people say that there
is only one right answer in math, but that

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:57.340
every answer is equally correct in literature.
That’s just bunk!

00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:03.409
First off, there is often more than one correct
answer in math, and secondly NOT EVERY ANSWER

00:02:03.409 --> 00:02:04.970
IS EQUALLY CORRECT IN LITERATURE! I am a giant
squid of anger!

00:02:04.970 --> 00:02:08.530
For instance, Hank, if you think that The
Adventures of Huckleberry Finn is a pro-slavery

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:12.550
novel, you’re wrong! You’re as wrong as
you are if you think that the square root

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:14.390
of four is strawberries!

00:02:14.390 --> 00:02:17.730
Right, so students of the world, I want you
to look at me for a second. Most of the questions

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.709
that you’re asking in your literature classes
are not that interesting. Like the question

00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:26.480
of whether the author intended that symbol
or that metaphor- let me tell you, as an author

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:30.080
who intends symbols and metaphors- that question
is not interesting.

00:02:30.080 --> 00:02:34.060
It doesn’t matter if, when I was writing
Paper Towns, I wanted the Great White Wall

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:38.330
of Cow to reference the Great White Wall of
Whale in Moby Dick. The Great White Wall of

00:02:38.330 --> 00:02:41.501
Cow and/or Whale isn’t there for you to
think about authorial intent, it’s there

00:02:41.501 --> 00:02:46.860
for you to think about nature’s seemingly
absolute apathy toward individual humans and

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:50.990
what the heroic response to that apathy is.
And the reason that reading critically, like

00:02:50.990 --> 00:02:54.890
reading for theme and metaphor and symbol
is important is because those things are ways

00:02:54.890 --> 00:02:59.569
in to the big, interesting questions, many
of which are same questions that math is trying

00:02:59.569 --> 00:03:00.569
to answer.

00:03:00.569 --> 00:03:04.180
Hank, the vast majority of us imagine ourselves
as like literature people or else as like

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:07.910
math people. But the truth is that the massive
parallel processor known as the human brain

00:03:07.910 --> 00:03:11.910
is neither a literature organ or a math organ.
It is both and more.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:14.910
Hank, putting aside learning for learning’s
sake, I’d argue that understanding the universe

00:03:14.910 --> 00:03:19.209
and its inhabitants is helpful in pretty much
every human endeavor, which is why nerds,

00:03:19.209 --> 00:03:22.989
in my experience, are more likely to understand
special relativity and also more likely to

00:03:22.989 --> 00:03:23.989
be good at kissing.

00:03:23.989 --> 00:03:27.280
So math people, let me tell you that imaginary
stories can be every bit as intellectually

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:32.150
engaging as imaginary numbers. And literature
people, I am here to tell you that set theory

00:03:32.150 --> 00:03:37.040
is every bit as fascinating and moving and
beautiful as the Great Gatsby.

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:43.720
Hank, I’ll see you on Monday. Is it Friday?
No, it’s Wednesday, isn’t it. Hank, I’ll

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:49.660
see you on Friday. God, I’m so bad at days!
But really Hank, are days of the week math

00:03:49.660 --> 00:03:50.690
or literature? They’re both.

